1
00:00:02,350 --> 00:00:05,955
The control channel is the one frequency
on a trunked system that never carries a

2
00:00:05,955 --> 00:00:09,560
voice — and it is the only one worth
decoding first.

3
00:00:09,910 --> 00:00:13,642
Tune across a trunked site and you will
find it immediately: a strong, continuous

4
00:00:13,642 --> 00:00:17,375
data carrier that never stops and never
speaks.

5
00:00:17,495 --> 00:00:19,945
That is the control channel — the system's
coordinator.

6
00:00:20,065 --> 00:00:23,508
It carries a nonstop stream of short
signalling messages: registrations, call

7
00:00:23,508 --> 00:00:26,950
requests, and above all, channel grants.

8
00:00:27,299 --> 00:00:30,376
Every message answers a question some
radio just asked.

9
00:00:30,496 --> 00:00:33,851
A radio powers on: the control channel
registers it.

10
00:00:33,971 --> 00:00:36,444
A user keys up: the control channel grants
the call and names the channel it will

11
00:00:36,444 --> 00:00:38,917
happen on.

12
00:00:39,037 --> 00:00:42,352
Because it announces where every call
goes, locking onto this one frequency

13
00:00:42,352 --> 00:00:45,666
makes the entire system's activity
visible.

14
00:00:46,017 --> 00:00:49,012
Its counterpart is the voice channel — or
traffic channel.

15
00:00:49,132 --> 00:00:52,795
Control carries data and coordinates; a
voice channel carries an actual

16
00:00:52,795 --> 00:00:56,458
conversation, and only for the brief life
of a single call.

17
00:00:56,578 --> 00:00:59,771
A grant points a talkgroup at a voice
channel, the audio flows, a short hang

18
00:00:59,771 --> 00:01:02,963
time keeps replies on the same channel,
and then the frequency goes idle and

19
00:01:02,963 --> 00:01:06,156
rejoins the pool.

20
00:01:06,506 --> 00:01:10,960
The messages themselves are compact,
fixed-format bursts, packed back to back

21
00:01:10,960 --> 00:01:15,415
on a fixed-rate downlink: TSBKs on P25,
CSBKs on DMR, and their equivalents

22
00:01:15,415 --> 00:01:19,869
elsewhere.

23
00:01:19,989 --> 00:01:23,398
Between grants, the stream also broadcasts
the system's identity, a list of neighbor

24
00:01:23,398 --> 00:01:26,808
sites so radios know where to roam, and
periodic status that keeps idle radios

25
00:01:26,808 --> 00:01:30,217
locked.

26
00:01:30,567 --> 00:01:32,239
And the announcements repeat.

27
00:01:32,359 --> 00:01:36,214
A radio that arrives mid-call — it was out
of range, or just powered on — hears an

28
00:01:36,214 --> 00:01:40,068
update for the call in progress and joins
late.

29
00:01:40,188 --> 00:01:43,386
That trick, late entry, only works because
the control channel keeps narrating the

30
00:01:43,386 --> 00:01:46,585
system's state over and over.

31
00:01:46,935 --> 00:01:51,684
Most systems dedicate one frequency to the
job permanently — P25, DMR Tier 3, TETRA —

32
00:01:51,684 --> 00:01:56,432
which is the easy case to find and
monitor.

33
00:01:56,552 --> 00:01:59,474
Lighter DMR modes rotate the control
function around the pool on a rest

34
00:01:59,474 --> 00:02:02,396
channel, and some systems interleave
control signalling with voice on the same

35
00:02:02,396 --> 00:02:05,318
carrier.

36
00:02:05,668 --> 00:02:08,245
For GopherTrunk, the control channel is
the map.

37
00:02:08,365 --> 00:02:12,266
Decode it, and every call on the system
announces itself before it starts.

38
00:02:12,386 --> 00:02:16,020
GopherTrunk parses the grant, affiliation,
and system messages in real time, tasks a

39
00:02:16,020 --> 00:02:19,654
receiver at each granted voice channel,
and follows many conversations from one

40
00:02:19,654 --> 00:02:23,288
wideband capture.

41
00:02:23,408 --> 00:02:26,288
Finding an unknown system works the same
way: hunt for the carrier that is always

42
00:02:26,288 --> 00:02:29,167
on and never talks.

43
00:02:30,317 --> 00:02:34,160
So: the control channel is the data-only
coordinator of a trunked system.

44
00:02:34,280 --> 00:02:37,821
It announces every grant, registration,
and system parameter.

45
00:02:37,941 --> 00:02:40,530
Decode it first — it is the map to
everything else.

46
00:02:40,650 --> 00:02:42,159
Full write-up linked below.
